From dae4d562c718e98b0fcc4ea86bd25e4dbb807b61 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joshua Warren <816217+joshuaswarren@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 15:45:11 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] mem_mgr: handle 16K system memory page size

On kernels configured with a 16K page size, the default system memory
page size picked by _memmgrPickDefaultSysmemPageSize is the OS page size
(16384). The page size switch at the end of memmgrDeterminePageSize only
knows 4K, 64K, 128K, 2M, 512M and 256G, so a 16K value fell through to
the default case, made the function return 0, and every RM init path
that allocates system memory through it (channel, scrubber and ce_utils
buffers among them) failed with NV_ERR_INVALID_STATE:
"memmgrDeterminePageSize failed, status 0x40".

Recognise the 16K OS page size: keep returning it so callers size and
align to the OS page, but report the 4K PTE page size attribute, since
the GPU walks the allocation with small pages. 4K, 64K-page and larger
kernels take exactly the same path as before.

Observed on a GeForce RTX 5060 Ti (GB206) in an arm64 kernel with 16K
pages, 595.112.07 branch with the companion nv-linux submap patch applied:

    NVRM: GPU0 memmgrAllocResources_IMPL: memmgrDeterminePageSize failed, status: 0x40
    NVRM: GPU0 RmInitNvDevice: *** Cannot load state into the device
    NVRM: GPU 0001:03:00.0: RmInitAdapter failed! (0x25:0x40:1373)

Tested: the same card on a 16-inch M1 Max MacBook Pro over a USB4 PCIe
tunnel with this change: nvidia-smi lists the card, a CUDA context and a
1 GiB allocation succeed, and llama.cpp inference runs on it. A stock
module without either patch was not run.

Signed-off-by: Joshua Warren <816217+joshuaswarren@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 src/nvidia/inc/kernel/gpu/mem_mgr/rm_page_size.h | 1 +
 src/nvidia/src/kernel/gpu/mem_mgr/mem_mgr.c      | 9 +++++++++
 2 files changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/nvidia/inc/kernel/gpu/mem_mgr/rm_page_size.h b/src/nvidia/inc/kernel/gpu/mem_mgr/rm_page_size.h
index f25795eac9..9639255d8c 100644
--- a/src/nvidia/inc/kernel/gpu/mem_mgr/rm_page_size.h
+++ b/src/nvidia/inc/kernel/gpu/mem_mgr/rm_page_size.h
@@ -36,6 +36,7 @@
 //---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 #define RM_PAGE_SIZE_INVALID 0
 #define RM_PAGE_SIZE         4096
+#define RM_PAGE_SIZE_16K     (16 * 1024)
 #define RM_PAGE_SIZE_64K     (64 * 1024)
 #define RM_PAGE_SIZE_128K    (128 * 1024)
 #define RM_PAGE_MASK         0x0FFF
diff --git a/src/nvidia/src/kernel/gpu/mem_mgr/mem_mgr.c b/src/nvidia/src/kernel/gpu/mem_mgr/mem_mgr.c
index 73bebf9740..c3ce8384f8 100644
--- a/src/nvidia/src/kernel/gpu/mem_mgr/mem_mgr.c
+++ b/src/nvidia/src/kernel/gpu/mem_mgr/mem_mgr.c
@@ -1957,6 +1957,15 @@ memmgrDeterminePageSize_IMPL
             *pRetAttr = FLD_SET_DRF(OS32, _ATTR, _PAGE_SIZE, _BIG, *pRetAttr);
             break;
 
+        //
+        // On kernels that use a 16K page size, system memory is carved at the
+        // OS page size but is walked with 4K PTEs, so report the small-page
+        // attribute while returning the OS page size for alignment and sizing.
+        //
+        case RM_PAGE_SIZE_16K:
+            *pRetAttr = FLD_SET_DRF(OS32, _ATTR, _PAGE_SIZE, _4KB, *pRetAttr);
+            break;
+
         case RM_PAGE_SIZE_HUGE:
             *pRetAttr = FLD_SET_DRF(OS32, _ATTR, _PAGE_SIZE, _HUGE, *pRetAttr);
             *pRetAttr2 = FLD_SET_DRF(OS32, _ATTR2, _PAGE_SIZE_HUGE, _2MB, *pRetAttr2);
